## The SoX Resampler Library (libsoxr) — LGPL-2.1-or-later

mint's only sample-rate conversion backend is libsoxr, a C library:

- **Copyright** (c) 2007–18 Rob Sykes &lt;robs@users.sourceforge.net&gt;
- **Licence** GNU Lesser General Public License, version 2.1 or later
- **Version used for releases** 0.1.3
- **Corresponding source** `https://deb.debian.org/debian/pool/main/libs/libsoxr/libsoxr_0.1.3.orig.tar.xz`
  SHA-256 `b111c15fdc8c029989330ff559184198c161100a59312f5dc19ddeb9b5a15889`
  This exact archive is attached to every GitHub release of mint.

mint binds libsoxr's C API directly (`src/ops/soxr.rs`) rather than through the `libsoxr` /
`libsoxr-sys` crates it used to depend on. Those were LGPL-2.0 *Rust* code and were
statically linked, so they carried the same obligation into the binary that dynamic linking
removes for the C library. **This binary now contains no LGPL code of any kind.**

**How the C library is linked, and why that matters.** libsoxr is linked **dynamically** on
every platform. On macOS and Linux the released binaries link the copy already installed on your
system (`brew install libsoxr`, `apt install libsoxr0`) and mint distributes no part of the
library at all. On Windows there is no system package, so `soxr.dll` is shipped inside the
archive next to `mint.exe`; you may replace that DLL with any interface-compatible build of
your own and mint will use it. Either way you can modify libsoxr and use your modified
version with mint without rebuilding mint — which is the freedom LGPL-2.1 §6 exists to
preserve, and the reason mint stopped linking it statically.

The Windows `soxr.dll` is produced by vcpkg's `soxr:x64-windows` port; vcpkg's own copyright
notice for it is shipped alongside as `soxr-COPYRIGHT.txt`, and the port (including any
patches vcpkg applies on top of upstream 0.1.3) is at
`https://github.com/microsoft/vcpkg/tree/master/ports/soxr`.

## Symphonia — MPL-2.0

The audio demuxers and decoders are Symphonia, under the Mozilla Public License 2.0. MPL-2.0
is file-level copyleft: mint has not modified any Symphonia file, and the Source Code Form of
every MPL-covered file is available at `https://github.com/pdeljanov/Symphonia` and on
crates.io at the versions listed below.

## MP3

Released binaries **do not** contain MP3 support and do not link libmp3lame. MP3 output is an
opt-in build feature (`cargo build --features mp3`) precisely so that released artefacts carry
no LAME code; `mint --version` states which kind of build you have.

